              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "soured-cream",
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": "use 300ml buttermilk -- or mix 300ml whole milk with 1 tbsp lemon juice and leave for 5 minutes",
              "amount": 300,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 200C. Lightly flour a baking sheet."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Sift the flour, bicarbonate of soda, and salt into a large bowl. Make a well in the centre."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour in the buttermilk. Mix with a fork or your hands until the dough just comes together. It should be soft but not sticky. Add a splash more buttermilk if it feels dry."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Turn onto a floured surface and pat into a round about 4cm thick. Place on the prepared baking sheet. Cut a deep cross into the top with a sharp knife."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bake for 35 to 40 minutes until the loaf sounds hollow when tapped underneath. Cool on a wire rack and eat within a few hours of baking."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Bread has a strong soapy taste",
              "cause": "Too much bicarbonate of soda.",
              "fix": "Level your teaspoon carefully. Too much bicarb is more damaging here than too little."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Centre is gummy after baking",
              "cause": "Not baked through. The cross helps, but a thick loaf still needs full time.",
              "fix": "Make the loaf no more than 4cm thick, and check by tapping the base -- it must sound hollow."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Soda bread is rooted in Irish farmhouse cooking. It spread to Scotland and England as a quick everyday bread. Before commercial yeast was widely available, baking soda and buttermilk were common leaveners in home kitchens. This is a bread that asks very little of you and delivers fresh bread in under an hour."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
